page cmd_lab {
  title = "vmlab lab"
  chapter_header "vmlab lab" {
    kicker       = "Commands · reference"
    reading_time = "6 min read"
    updated      = "2026-09-02"
    version      = "vmlab 0.9"
  }

  p <<DOC
    `vmlab lab` manages running labs host-wide, by name rather than by the
    current directory. The supervisor keeps a registry of every lab daemon
    it started, and these verbs read it and act on the daemons behind it. See
    [How vmlab runs a lab](guide_architecture) for the supervisor and the
    per-lab daemons.
  DOC

  code sh { source = "vmlab lab <COMMAND>" }

  table {
    rows:
      | "Subcommand" | "Meaning" |
      | "`list`" | "List every tracked lab: name, state, and directory." |
      | "`info`" | "Show detailed status (machines and segments) of a running lab." |
      | "`stop`" | "Gracefully stop a running lab; clones retained." |
      | "`restart`" | "Restart a lab's daemon so it re-reads `vmlab.wcl`." |
      | "`destroy`" | "Stop a lab and delete its clones and local state." |
      | "`-h`, `--help`" | "Print help." |
  }

  p <<DOC
    The read-only subcommands never start the supervisor: with none running
    the registry is empty and `list` says so. None of them starts a lab
    daemon either; a lab that is not running is reported, not brought up.
  DOC

  h2 "vmlab lab list"

  code sh { source = "vmlab lab list [OPTIONS]" }

  table {
    rows:
      | "Option" | "Meaning" |
      | "`--json`" | "Emit a JSON array instead of a table." |
      | "`-h`, `--help`" | "Print help." |
  }

  p <<DOC
    Prints one row per registered lab with `NAME`, `STATE` and `DIRECTORY`.
    The state is `running`, `stopping` or `failed`, the last meaning the
    daemon exited without being asked to. With no supervisor or an empty
    registry it prints `no running labs`. Under `--json` each entry carries
    `name`, `root`, `pid` and `state`.
  DOC

  h2 "vmlab lab info"

  code sh { source = "vmlab lab info [OPTIONS] <LAB>" }

  table {
    rows:
      | "Option" | "Meaning" |
      | "`<LAB>`" | "The lab's name." |
      | "`-v`, `--verbose`" | "Add the raw power state, readiness, and each machine's kind-specific detail." |
      | "`-h`, `--help`" | "Print help." |
  }

  p <<DOC
    The host-wide form of [vmlab status](cmd_status). With the daemon
    reachable it prints `directory: <root>` and then the same report `status`
    prints, with the same `--verbose` detail. A lab that is registered but
    whose daemon does not answer, for example one in the `failed` state,
    prints one line from the registry, `lab "<name>" [<state>] (not reachable)
    directory <root>`, and exits 0. A name the registry does not know fails
    with `lab "<name>" is not running`.
  DOC

  h2 "vmlab lab stop"

  code sh { source = "vmlab lab stop [OPTIONS] <LAB>" }

  table {
    rows:
      | "Option" | "Meaning" |
      | "`<LAB>`" | "The lab's name." |
      | "`--force`" | "Hard kill instead of the graceful ladder." |
      | "`-h`, `--help`" | "Print help." |
  }

  p <<DOC
    Stops every machine in the named lab through the graceful ladder
    [vmlab down](cmd_down) describes, or kills them under `--force`, and
    keeps the clones. Prints `lab "<name>" is down (clones retained)`. A lab
    with no reachable daemon prints `lab "<name>" is not running` and exits 0;
    unlike `down` this form reaps no orphaned processes, since without the
    registry entry it has no directory to release.
  DOC

  h2 "vmlab lab restart"

  code sh { source = "vmlab lab restart [OPTIONS] <LAB>" }

  table {
    rows:
      | "Option" | "Meaning" |
      | "`<LAB>`" | "The lab's name." |
      | "`--json`" | "Emit the raw JSON reply instead of a confirmation." |
      | "`-h`, `--help`" | "Print help." |
  }

  p <<DOC
    Replaces the lab's daemon so it re-reads `vmlab.wcl`. This is not
    `down` followed by `up`: that stops every machine and re-runs
    provisioning, whereas this replaces only the daemon. It is the way to
    pick up an edit to the [lab file](guide_lab_file), and the way to recover
    a daemon whose lab file no longer loads.
  DOC

  p <<DOC
    The lab must already be stopped. A fresh daemon cannot re-adopt machines
    the old one was running, and the old daemon's own shutdown stops them, so
    a lab with machines still running is refused with `lab "<name>" still has
    machines running — stop them first; a restarted daemon cannot re-adopt
    them`. A daemon that cannot answer a status request at all is not a
    veto, because a lab whose file no longer loads is exactly what this verb
    exists to recover.
  DOC

  p <<DOC
    Run inside the lab's own directory, the current directory is the root
    the supervisor is asked to restart from; run elsewhere, the registry's
    entry is used. The supervisor refuses a name already registered from a
    different directory. On success the verb pings the new daemon and
    prints `lab "<name>" daemon restarted at <socket>`; under `--json` it
    prints the supervisor's reply, which carries the new `socket` path.
  DOC

  h2 "vmlab lab destroy"

  code sh { source = "vmlab lab destroy <LAB>" }

  table {
    rows:
      | "Option" | "Meaning" |
      | "`<LAB>`" | "The lab's name." |
      | "`-h`, `--help`" | "Print help." |
  }

  p <<DOC
    The host-wide form of [vmlab destroy](cmd_destroy): withdraws the lab's
    SSH aliases, asks the daemon to stop every machine and delete the clones,
    volumes and lab-local state, and releases the lab at the supervisor so
    its daemon is reaped. With no reachable daemon but a registry entry, it
    removes the lab's `.vmlab/` directory itself. A name the registry does
    not know fails with `lab "<name>" is not running`. Prints `lab "<name>"
    destroyed`.
  DOC

  h2 "Examples"

  p "See what is running on this host:"
  code sh { source = "vmlab lab list" }

  code sh { source = <<'SRC'
NAME      STATE      DIRECTORY
ad-lab    running    /home/wil/labs/ad-lab
mixed-lab failed     /home/wil/labs/mixed-lab
SRC
  }

  p "Pick up an edited lab file without re-provisioning:"
  code sh { source = <<'SRC'
vmlab lab stop ad-lab
vmlab lab restart ad-lab
vmlab up
SRC
  }

  p "Free the host of a lab you started from another directory:"
  code sh { source = "vmlab lab destroy peer-b" }

  h2 "Exit status"

  p <<DOC
    Exit status is 0 on success, including `list` with nothing running,
    `stop` on a lab that is not running, and `info` on a registered lab
    whose daemon does not answer. An unknown lab name, a lab with machines
    still running at `restart`, a daemon that fails the request, or a
    supervisor that does not come up exits 1 (`failed`). `restart` exits 5
    (`conflict`) when the supervisor already tracks this name from another
    directory. A usage error exits 2.
  DOC
}
